<Guide slug="zip-code-vs-postal-code" description="ZIP code vs. postal code: a ZIP code is the US form of a postal code. See formats from 10 countries and what to enter on international forms." faqs={faqs}>
  <h1>ZIP code vs. postal code</h1>
  <p class="lead" data-answer><strong>A ZIP code is a postal code — specifically, the one used in the United States.</strong> “Postal code” is the general term used worldwide; each country has its own name and format. So every ZIP code is a postal code, but most postal codes are not ZIP codes.</p>

  <h2>How the US ZIP code compares</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Format:</strong> five digits, optionally followed by four more (<a href="/guides/what-is-zip-plus-4/">ZIP+4</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Characters:</strong> numbers only.</li>
    <li><strong>Run by:</strong> the United States Postal Service.</li>
    <li><strong>Covers:</strong> the 50 states, DC, US territories and military mail.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Postal codes around the world</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>Name</th><th>Format</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{rows.map((r) => <tr>{r.map((c) => <td>{c}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Filling in forms</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>US addresses:</strong> enter the five-digit ZIP code; add the +4 if you know it.</li>
    <li><strong>International mail from the US:</strong> write the destination country’s postal code exactly as the recipient gives it, and put the country name on the last line.</li>
    <li><strong>Building a form?</strong> Label the field “ZIP / postal code” and accept letters, spaces and hyphens.</li>
  </ul>
</Guide>
